module saAnalysis
module aminoAcidAnalysis

manifest MeOH
manifest HydrazineReagent
manifest AADerivatizationReagent
manifest AASolvent
manifest DBSPunch
manifest InternalStandard_13C5_SA

// ============================================================
// Dried Blood Spot Analysis by Digital Microfluidics Coupled
// to Nanoelectrospray Ionization Mass Spectrometry.
// Shih, Yang, Jebrail, Fobel, McIntosh, Al-Dirbashi,
// Chakraborty, Wheeler - Anal. Chem. 2012, 84, 3731-3738.
// DOI: 10.1021/ac300305s
//
// PROTOCOL OVERVIEW:
//   A 3.2 mm diameter dried blood spot (DBS) punch on Whatman
//   903 filter paper is loaded onto the DMF device bottom plate.
//   A nine-step automated protocol performs sequential solvent
//   extraction, derivatization, and nESI-MS/MS analysis of
//   succinylacetone (SA), a biomarker for hepatorenal tyrosinemia
//   (tyrosinemia type 1, HT).
//
//   Optionally, an extended protocol using a second nESI emitter
//   also enables parallel amino acid (AA) analysis from the
//   methanol pre-extractate (Fig. 6 in paper).
//
// KEY REAGENTS:
//   MeOH:               Neat methanol, pre-extraction solvent.
//   HydrazineReagent:   6.5 mM hydrazine + 15 uM 13C5-SA in
//                       80:20 acetonitrile:H2O + 0.1% v/v formic
//                       acid. SA extraction/derivatization/MS solvent.
//   AADerivatizationReagent: 3 N HCl in n-butanol, for amino acid
//                       butyl ester derivatization at 75 C.
//   AASolvent:          Acetonitrile:H2O 4:1 v/v, AA MS solvent.
//   InternalStandard_13C5_SA: 13C5-labeled succinylacetone (15 uM),
//                       co-dispensed in HydrazineReagent. Modelled
//                       as a separate manifest for clarity; in
//                       practice it is pre-mixed into HydrazineReagent.
//
// DROPLET VOLUMES: 15 uL per droplet for all steps.
//   Device gap: ~450 um (5x double-sided tape spacer).
//   Each 15 uL droplet spans approx. two 2.2 x 2.2 mm electrodes.
//
// ELECTRODE ACTUATION:
//   AC: 150-350 V_rms sine wave at 15 kHz (function generator +
//   amplifier). Automated impedance-based feedback control
//   (RBBB Arduino): if movement fails, voltage steps up 50 V_rms
//   (max 350 V_rms) until success. 100% movement success rate
//   achieved vs. 60% without feedback.
//
// nESI-MS INTERFACE:
//   Pulled-glass capillary emitter (360 um o.d., 50 um i.d.,
//   30 um tip i.d.) inserted between DMF plates. Droplet fills
//   capillary by capillary action (~100 nL capacity). DC 1.7-2.2 kV
//   applied to ITO top plate to generate stable nanoelectrospray
//   into LTQ mass spectrometer (Thermo Scientific).
//   Spray stable for >200 s, RSD = 7.3%, consumption ~800 nL/min.
//
// MS/MS DETECTION:
//   SA derivatized with hydrazine -> hydrazone derivative.
//   Parent ion -> 25% CID -> neutral loss of H2O (18 m/z).
//   SA: m/z 155 -> 137. 13C5-SA (IS): m/z 160 -> 142.
//   Spectra averaged over 50 acquisitions.
//   Calibration range: 5-80 uM SA in DBS punches.
//   R^2 = 0.9923. LOD: 4.95 uM (2.43 ng). Recovery: 85.2%.
//   HT clinical range: 16-150 uM.
//   Validated vs. NSO gold standard (paired t-test, p=0.05,
//   t=0.8177 -> no significant difference at 95% CI, Table 2).
//
// TEMPERATURE CONTROL:
//   Step 7 (SA extraction incubation): 37 C on hot-plate until
//   evaporation (~15 min). Modelled as heat at 37c for 15m.
//   AA derivatization step: 75 C until dryness (~15 min).
//   Modelled as heat at 75c for 15m.
//
// NOTE ON DBS ABSTRACTION:
//   The DBS punch is a solid-phase object placed on the DMF
//   device bottom plate. `mix` operations model the actuation
//   of a liquid droplet across and around the DBS punch (circular
//   5-electrode actuation pattern). The punch is not dispensed
//   from a reservoir; it is pre-loaded before the protocol begins.
//   DBSPunch is declared as a manifest to allow BioScript to
//   reference the punch location in mix steps.
//
// NOTE ON COMPILER CONSTRAINTS (lilott8/BioScript):
//   - Duration tokens: s (seconds) and m (minutes) only.
//   - `heat` models both hot-plate incubation and evaporation steps.
//   - No multi-unit dispense arrays are used; all droplets are
//     scalar 1-unit operations to avoid stranded array elements.
//   - Waste removal after each MeOH step modelled as `drain`.
//   - The nESI spray and MS detection are modelled as `detect`
//     calls using the saAnalysis / aminoAcidAnalysis modules.
//
// CFG STRUCTURE:
//   BB1  main             -- entry: pre-extraction (steps 1-6),
//                           SA extraction/derivatization (steps 7-8),
//                           nESI-MS analysis (step 9), AA branch
//   BB2  saPass           -- saSignal > 0: SA detected in range;
//                           dispose derivatized droplet
//   BB3  saFail           -- saSignal <= 0: signal below LOD or
//                           extraction failed; drain
//   BB4  saJoin           -- join: AA analysis branch begins
//   BB5  aaPass           -- aaSignal > 0: amino acids detected;
//                           dispose AA droplet
//   BB6  aaFail           -- aaSignal <= 0: AA signal invalid; drain
//   BB7  finalCleanup     -- terminal: dispose all QC signals
// ============================================================

instructions:

// ============================================================
// BB1: main
// ============================================================

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 1: MeOH pre-extraction #1 (15 min, room temperature).
//   A 15 uL droplet of neat methanol is loaded into the device
//   reservoir by pipetting at the plate edge and applying
//   driving potential to draw it in. The droplet is then
//   actuated to the DBS punch position and incubated for 15 min.
//   Methanol extracts amino acids, acylcarnitines, and other
//   small molecules that diffuse out of the blood matrix into
//   the solvent. SA remains in the residual blood spot at
//   this stage (it is extracted by hydrazine in step 7).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

meoh1        = dispense 1 units of MeOH
dbsPunch1    = dispense 1 units of DBSPunch
preExtract1  = mix meoh1 with dbsPunch1 for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2: Actuate MeOH pre-extractate #1 to waste reservoir.
//   After incubation, the methanol droplet (now containing
//   extracted amino acids and acylcarnitines) is actuated
//   away from the DBS punch to a waste reservoir electrode.
//   The DBS punch remains on the bottom plate.
//   In the extended AA-analysis protocol (Fig. 6), this
//   extractate is NOT wasted but instead isolated for
//   derivatization; see STEP 2a/2b branch below.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

drain preExtract1

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 3: MeOH pre-extraction #2 (15 min, room temperature).
//   A fresh 15 uL MeOH droplet is dispensed and actuated to
//   the residual DBS punch for a second pre-extraction cycle.
//   Three sequential methanol pre-extractions are performed
//   to maximise removal of compounds that would otherwise
//   interfere with the SA hydrazine derivatization step.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

meoh2        = dispense 1 units of MeOH
preExtract2  = mix meoh2 with dbsPunch1 for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 4: Actuate MeOH pre-extractate #2 to waste.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

drain preExtract2

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 5: MeOH pre-extraction #3 (15 min, room temperature).
//   Third and final methanol pre-extraction cycle.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

meoh3        = dispense 1 units of MeOH
preExtract3  = mix meoh3 with dbsPunch1 for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 6: Actuate MeOH pre-extractate #3 to waste.
//   After three pre-extraction cycles, the DBS punch retains
//   succinylacetone (SA), which is not efficiently extracted
//   by methanol. The residual punch is now ready for SA
//   extraction with hydrazine reagent.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

drain preExtract3

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 7: SA extraction and derivatization - Part A (37 C,
//   15 min to evaporation).
//   A 15 uL droplet of the SA extraction/derivatization/MS
//   solvent (6.5 mM hydrazine + 15 uM 13C5-SA internal
//   standard in 80:20 acetonitrile:H2O + 0.1% formic acid)
//   is loaded and actuated to the residual DBS punch.
//   The device is placed on a hot-plate at 37 C. The droplet
//   incubates until evaporation (~15 min). Hydrazine reacts
//   with SA to form a stable hydrazone derivative. The
//   evaporation step concentrates the derivatized analyte.
//   Reaction mechanism: see Sander et al. Clin. Chem. 2006.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

hydrazineReagent1 = dispense 1 units of HydrazineReagent
is1               = dispense 1 units of InternalStandard_13C5_SA
extractionMix1    = mix hydrazineReagent1 with is1 for 5s
saExtraction1     = mix extractionMix1 with dbsPunch1 for 15m

// Incubate at 37 C until evaporation (modelled as 15 min heat).
heat saExtraction1 at 37c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 8: SA extraction and derivatization - Part B (1 min,
//   room temperature, circular actuation).
//   A fresh 15 uL droplet of the same SA extraction/
//   derivatization/MS solvent is loaded and delivered to the
//   residual (now evaporated) blood spot. The droplet is
//   incubated for 1 min at room temperature. During this
//   minute, the feedback-controlled electrode system actuates
//   the droplet in a circular pattern around the five
//   electrodes adjacent to the DBS punch to maximise
//   re-dissolution and collection of derivatized SA hydrazone.
//   This two-step extraction (evaporate then re-dissolve)
//   is analogous to the conventional method (Allard et al.
//   Clin. Biochem. 2004) but miniaturised onto DMF.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

hydrazineReagent2 = dispense 1 units of HydrazineReagent
is2               = dispense 1 units of InternalStandard_13C5_SA
extractionMix2    = mix hydrazineReagent2 with is2 for 5s

// Circular actuation around DBS punch for 1 min re-dissolution.
// `mix` abstracts the 5-electrode circular actuation sequence.
derivatizedSA     = mix extractionMix2 with saExtraction1 for 1m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 9: Actuate derivatized SA droplet to nESI capillary
//   emitter inlet and acquire MS/MS data.
//   The droplet is driven to the edge of the pulled-glass
//   capillary emitter entrance orifice. The capillary fills
//   by capillary action (~100 nL capacity, seconds).
//   DC voltage (1.7-2.2 kV) is applied to the ITO top plate
//   to generate a stable nanoelectrospray. MS/MS spectra
//   are collected (50 acquisitions averaged) for SA
//   quantification via neutral loss (m/z 155->137 for SA,
//   160->142 for 13C5-SA). The saAnalysis module abstracts
//   the LTQ MS/MS acquisition and peak ratio calculation.
// ------------------------------------------------------------

saSignal = detect saAnalysis on derivatizedSA for 200s

// ============================================================
// BB2 / BB3: SA quantification QC gate
// Pass (BB2): saSignal > 0 -> SA detected above LOD (4.95 uM);
//             ratio SA/13C5-SA used for calibration curve
//             quantification; dispose processed droplet.
// Fail (BB3): saSignal <= 0 -> SA below LOD or spray failed;
//             drain processed droplet.
// Both branches converge at BB4 (saJoin).
// ============================================================

if (saSignal > 0) {
    dispose derivatizedSA
} else {
    drain derivatizedSA
}

// ============================================================
// BB4: saJoin
// Dispose SA QC signal. Control falls into the extended
// amino acid (AA) analysis branch (BB5/BB6).
//
// NOTE ON AA BRANCH:
//   In the main protocol (SA-only), the methanol pre-extractates
//   (steps 1-6) are discarded. In the extended protocol (Fig. 6),
//   a 5 min (not 15 min) first MeOH incubation is used, and the
//   extractate is diverted for AA derivatization with n-butanol
//   HCl at 75 C before nESI-MS via a second capillary emitter.
//   This branch models the extended AA protocol using the same
//   DBS punch object as a second dispense to represent the
//   isolated MeOH extractate that would have been collected
//   in step 2 (rather than drained). In the BioScript model,
//   a fresh DBSPunch dispense represents the conceptual
//   isolation of this extractate for downstream AA processing.
// ============================================================

dispose saSignal

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2a (AA extended protocol): Dry the MeOH pre-extractate.
//   The MeOH extractate from the first incubation is actuated
//   to a non-reservoir electrode and evaporated to dryness
//   at room temperature (~15 min). This concentrates the amino
//   acids extracted from the DBS punch prior to derivatization.
//   Modelled as heat at 25 C for 15 min (ambient evaporation).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

meoh_aa      = dispense 1 units of MeOH
dbsPunch2    = dispense 1 units of DBSPunch

// 5 min MeOH incubation for AA extraction (shorter than SA protocol).
aaExtract    = mix meoh_aa with dbsPunch2 for 5m

// Evaporate to dryness at room temperature.
heat aaExtract at 25c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2b (AA extended protocol): Derivatize amino acids.
//   A 15 uL droplet of AA derivatization reagent (3 N HCl
//   in n-butanol) is loaded and actuated to the dried MeOH
//   extractate. Incubated at 75 C on hot-plate until dryness.
//   This forms n-butyl ester derivatives of amino acids
//   compatible with ESI-MS/MS analysis (standard NBS method,
//   analogous to Turgeon et al. Clin. Chem. 2008).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

aaDerivReagent   = dispense 1 units of AADerivatizationReagent
aaDerivMix       = mix aaDerivReagent with aaExtract for 5s

// Incubate at 75 C until dryness (butylation reaction).
heat aaDerivMix at 75c for 15m

// ------------------------------------------------------------
// STEP 2c (AA extended protocol): Re-dissolve derivatized AAs.
//   A 15 uL droplet of AA MS solvent (acetonitrile:H2O 4:1 v/v)
//   is loaded and driven to the dried butyl-ester derivative
//   to re-dissolve it, then actuated to the second nESI
//   capillary emitter (positioned at the opposite end of the
//   two-emitter device, Fig. 6a). nESI-MS data are collected
//   using the same 1.7-2.2 kV DC spray parameters.
//   AAs identified include: VAL, MET, TYR, HIS, GLN, PRO,
//   PHE, ASP (Fig. 6b, primary MS spectrum).
// ------------------------------------------------------------

aaSolventDrop    = dispense 1 units of AASolvent
aaReadySample    = mix aaSolventDrop with aaDerivMix for 1m

aaSignal = detect aminoAcidAnalysis on aaReadySample for 200s

// ============================================================
// BB5 / BB6: AA analysis QC gate
// Pass (BB5): aaSignal > 0 -> amino acid peaks detected;
//             dispose AA droplet.
// Fail (BB6): aaSignal <= 0 -> no AA signal (derivatization
//             or spray failed); drain.
// Both branches converge at BB7 (finalCleanup).
// ============================================================

if (aaSignal > 0) {
    dispose aaReadySample
} else {
    drain aaReadySample
}

// ============================================================
// BB7: finalCleanup (terminal)
// Dispose all QC signal droplets. Experiment ends.
// ============================================================

dispose aaSignal

// [terminal -- no further instructions; exit node for CFG]
